# Who has to look at what.
#
# Two jobs. GitHub auto-requests review from the owner of every path a pull
# request touches, and -- once the master ruleset turns on code-owner review
# (see docs/branch-protection.md) -- makes that review mandatory rather than
# advisory.
#
# Until then this file is a declaration, not a gate. It is still worth having
# committed: the list of paths that make up the control plane is the thing
# being agreed, and agreeing it is a separate act from enforcing it.
#
# Ordering matters -- the LAST matching pattern wins, so the broad default is
# first and the sensitive paths come after it.

# Everything, by default.
*                                       @Danathar

# --- the control plane ----------------------------------------------------
# Changes here alter what the other gates can do, so they are exactly the ones
# that must not be able to ride through on an ordinary review. An agent that
# can edit the workflow that checks it has no constraint; see
# docs/SECURITY-AI.md, "Disable or edit the security boundary itself".

# CI, release, and every other automation that holds a token.
/.github/workflows/                     @Danathar

# The ruleset definition, and this file. Both describe the boundary, so both
# are inside it.
/.github/rulesets/                      @Danathar
/.github/CODEOWNERS                     @Danathar

# What agents are permitted to do, and the mechanical half of it.
/docs/SECURITY-AI.md                    @Danathar
/docs/branch-protection.md              @Danathar
/AGENTS.md                              @Danathar
/.claude/settings.json                  @Danathar

# Change classification feeds the labels a reviewer triages by.
/.github/risk-tiers.yml                 @Danathar

